WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.360
"The Best Milk Alternative: Oat vs. Soy Milk"

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.760
Plant-based milks
continue to expand their share

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.760
of the "milk" market.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.160
Some see this shift
as a "palatable disruption,"

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.280
where people are encouraged

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.040
to care enough
about the environment,

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.880
health, and animal welfare
to adopt plant-based milks

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.520
but ultimately remain consumers
of a commodity food.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.160
Do people even need
to consume milk,

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.720
whether plant-based or dairy?

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.680
Recent guidelines from

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.360
the Academy of Nutrition
and Dietetics

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.960
recommend that one-
to two-year-old children

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.120
consume whole cow's milk,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.960
explicitly stating
that plant milks

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.000
should be avoided
with the exception of soy milk.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.400
This triggered an outcry
in the medical community

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.760
with significant concerns
about dairy industry influence

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.120
and the lack of data showing
cow's milk offers any benefit

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.840
over plant-based milks
to young children.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.640
Dr. Greger covered
this controversy in detail.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.560
After weaning, milk of any kind
is not required and, in fact,

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.080
should not be relied upon
as a main source of calories.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.160
Water is fine
provided it is combined

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.120
with a healthy, balanced diet.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.080
U.S. federal nutrition policy
and dairy industry promotions

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:15.920
have long recognized
dairy milks

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.360
as a high-quality source
of protein and calcium.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.080
However, although cow's milk
is rich in calcium,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.120
it does not appear
to reduce bone fractures.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.000
What's more,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.800
consumption of cow's milk
carries its own risks

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.360
and must be compared against
risks of plant-based milks,

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.120
which are primarily
only problematic

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.560
when they are used
inappropriately

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.480
in otherwise unbalanced diets

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.920
that provide
inadequate solid foods.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.640
Some milk products,
whether dairy or plant-based,

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.600
are fortified with vitamin D
or vitamin A.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.120
Plant-based milk alternatives

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.920
could benefit from
nutrient density standards,

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.000
particularly
if individuals rely on them

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.720
as sources of protein
and calcium.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.480
In a survey
of plant-based milk alternatives

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:57.760
in the U.S. market,

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.440
only soy milks served
as a "good" source of protein,

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.840
with around 7g per cup (240 ml)

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.080
and were consistently fortified.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.440
Most other plant-based milks,
like almond, rice, coconut,

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.360
hemp, flaxseed,
and cashew "milks,"

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.480
are not great options
for toddlers and young children.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.240
They have
inadequate nutrient profiles

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.240
to meet needs for protein,
calcium, and vitamin D.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.840
Other fortified
plant-based milks,

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.080
like pea milk
and possibly oat milk,

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.400
might be more reasonable options
for young children

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.120
who need an alternative
to soy milk.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.200
But not everyone agrees;

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.920
others recommend that other
fortified plant beverages,

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.000
like oat or almond milk,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.640
are only okay
for older children.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.680
Globally, soy milk has dominated
the plant-milk market,

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.840
making up more than 50%
of global plant-milk production.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.880
But oat milk's popularity
continues to grow steadily,

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.320
outperforming others like
almond milk and coconut milk.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.360
Oat milk came to the U.S. market
about 30 years ago,

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:56.240
but only recently,

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.040
with the arrival
of the Swedish brand Oatly

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.440
and its strategic
marketing campaign,

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.600
did oat milk
start overtaking soy milk

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.760
as the #2 plant-based milk
sold in the United States.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.440
In Europe, oat milk has become

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.320
the #1 most widely-consumed
plant milk.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.720
With more oat milk appearing
in refrigerator cases

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.480
at grocery stores,

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.040
how does it compare
to other plant milks,

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.280
particularly
NutritionFacts-recommended

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.360
unsweetened soy milk?

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.200
In terms of calcium
and vitamin D,

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.480
oat and soy milks have
similar amounts to cow's milk.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.520
Soy surpasses oat
in terms of protein content,

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.640
and soy milk is generally lower
in added sugar,

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.880
but there is
considerable variation,

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.840
with unsweetened
and very sweetened options

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.080
on the market.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.560
An important caveat to note
is that fortification

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.600
of plant-based milks
can differ by global region.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.600
For example,
in the United States,

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:53.120
about 80% of plant-based milks
are fortified with vitamin D,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.760
but only about 20%
are in Australia

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.680
and just 50% in Europe.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.480
As some people rely on milk for
calcium and vitamin D sources,

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.840
this is your reminder to check
the nutrition labels,

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.800
whether for added sugar content
or fortification,

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.440
so you know exactly
what you are or are not getting.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.880
What happens when you put oats
to the test for cholesterol?

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.800
People's LDL
can drop 10 points

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.040
from eating two small servings
of oatmeal a day

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.400
for eight weeks.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.240
Oat-based cold cereals like
Cheerios appear to also work,

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.040
dropping LDL around 7 points
more than control

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.120
in the same time frame.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:34.160
Does oat milk
have similar effects?

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:37.280
You don't know, until
it has been put to the test.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.560
Oat milk versus soy milk
versus cow's milk.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.320
People drank 3 to 4 cups
(720–960 ml) a day

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.800
of the respective milk
for 4 weeks,

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.040
and those who drank oat milk
or soy milk

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.880
had a significant drop in LDL,
around 8 points,

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.720
but drinking cow's milk
had no significant effect.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.040
A slightly larger trial
of about 50 men

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.200
had similar results
for oat milk,

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.600
an 8-point drop in LDL
over 5 weeks

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.400
for those drinking 3 cups
(720 ml) of oat milk per day,

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.160
versus no effect
in the control group

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.480
drinking rice milk.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:09.920
So, 3 cups of oat milk

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.400
has similar effects
on LDL cholesterol

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.280
as a cup and a half (360 ml)
of cooked oatmeal.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.080
Some of these
cholesterol-lowering effects

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.440
may be due to
the beta-glucan fiber in oats.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.200
Oats are one of the few foods,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.200
because of their
beta-glucan content,

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.160
that can have a "health claim"
for lowering heart disease risk

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.560
put on their packaging.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.920
But the soy protein
in soy milk can, too.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.480
And soy milk has more than
10 times the polyphenol content

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.320
of oat milk.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:40.080
Polyphenol-rich diets
have been associated

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.760
with a reduced risk
of chronic diseases,

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.600
like heart disease,
some cancers,

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.440
and Type 2 diabetes,

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:50.699
yet another reason
it is the healthier choice.

